In programming, **interpolation**, **`this`**, and **`for`** are common concepts, especially in JavaScript.

**Interpolation** inserts values into a string. JavaScript uses template literals, written with backticks:

```javascript
const name = "Maya";
console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);
```

The keyword **`this`** refers to the object associated with the current execution context:

```javascript
const user = {
  name: "Maya",
  greet() {
    console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}.`);
  }
};

user.greet();
```

A **`for`** loop repeats code a specified number of times or iterates over a collection:

```javascript
for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  console.log(`Iteration ${i}`);
}
```

Together, these features help developers produce dynamic text, access object properties, and perform repetitive tasks efficiently.
